<!--
    Kiswahili (sw-KE).

    Written for a Kenyan student audience, so the register is plain everyday Swahili
    rather than literary or bureaucratic. Kenyan usage is the reference: "chakula" for
    food, "kipika" for cooking pot, "chakula cha mchana" for lunch.

    The plural forms are not a copy of the English ones. Swahili marks "one" and "many"
    differently, and a literal "%d saved / %d zilizohifadhiwa" is wrong at n=1. Where the
    count is the noun's own count, this file supplies the idiomatic form (moja / %d).
    Where the count is a ratio or a percentage (recipe_checklist_progress, match_percent)
    it stays a single form, which is correct in Swahili.

    A native speaker should review this before release. It is written by someone who does
    not speak Swahili natively, and idiom is the one thing a translation cannot be
    mechanically correct about.
-->
